    <article id="fpane-transfiguration" class="fpane">
      <div class="icon-frame" data-icon-slot="transfiguration"></div>
      <h3>The Holy Transfiguration of Our Lord, God and Savior Jesus Christ</h3>
      <p class="feast-date">Thursday, August 6, 2026 · Fish, Oil and Wine Allowed</p>
      <p><a href="https://www.goarch.org/transfiguration" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GOARCH feast page</a> · <a href="https://www.goarch.org/chapel/calendar" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GOARCH calendar</a></p>

      <h4>The readings</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Matins Gospel — Luke 9:28-36</li>
        <li>Epistle — II Peter 1:10-19</li>
        <li>Gospel — Matthew 17:1-9</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small">GOARCH notes that the event is recorded in Matthew 17:1-9, Mark 9:2-8 and Luke 9:28-36.</p>

      <h4>The feast</h4>
      <p>Christ took Peter, James and John up a mountain, and there His appearance changed before them: His face shone and His clothing became brilliant. Moses and Elijah appeared and spoke with Him. Peter, overwhelmed, offered to build three shelters. A cloud overshadowed them and the voice of the Father was heard from it, declaring Christ to be His beloved Son.</p>
      <p>The Orthodox reading of the feast is that nothing was added to Christ on the mountain. What changed was the apostles — for a moment they were given to see the glory that was always His. The uncreated light of Tabor is the same light the Church prays to receive, and the reason the feast sits where it does in the fast is that the Church wants us climbing before we arrive.</p>

      <h4>Fasting on August 6</h4>
      <p>The GOARCH calendar designates August 6, 2026 as <strong>Fish, oil and wine are allowed. Refrain from meat, dairy and eggs.</strong> The fast is relaxed in honour of the feast, then resumes on August 7.</p>

      <h4>Customs, where practiced</h4>
      <p>In many parishes grapes and first fruits are brought to church and blessed after the Divine Liturgy on this day. Practice varies by parish and jurisdiction; ask your priest what your community does.</p>

      <div class="milestone-panel">
        <h3>A Day of Transfiguration, Baptism and Chrismation</h3>
        <p class="small">Saved on this device only. Nothing here is sent anywhere.</p>
        <div id="transfig-fields"></div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article id="fpane-dormition" class="fpane" hidden>
      <div class="icon-frame" data-icon-slot="dormition"></div>
      <h3>The Dormition of the Most Holy Theotokos</h3>
      <p class="feast-date">Saturday, August 15, 2026 · No fasting restrictions</p>
      <p><a href="https://www.goarch.org/dormition" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GOARCH feast page</a> · <a href="https://www.goarch.org/chapel/calendar" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GOARCH calendar</a></p>

      <h4>The word</h4>
      <p>Dormition translates the Greek <em>Koimesis</em> — a falling asleep. The Greek word for cemetery comes from the same root. The name is the theology: for those in Christ, death has become sleep.</p>

      <h4>The readings</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Matins Gospel — Luke 1:39-49, 56</li>
        <li>Epistle — Philippians 2:5-11</li>
        <li>Gospel — Luke 10:38-42; 11:27-28</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>The feast</h4>
      <p>The Church keeps the death of the Theotokos as a feast. She died as every human being dies, and her Son received her. The Church holds that she was then taken up bodily — this belongs to the Church's Tradition and to her hymnography rather than to a narrative in Scripture, and Orthodox teaching says so plainly rather than reading it into a biblical text.</p>
      <p>The distinction matters and is worth stating carefully: the Orthodox Church does not teach the feast in the terms the Roman Catholic Church defined in 1950, and it does not treat the account as a scriptural narrative. What it celebrates is that the first and closest disciple of Christ has already arrived where the whole Church is going.</p>
      <p>That is why fourteen days of fasting end here rather than in mourning. The fast prepares the household for a funeral that turns out to be a feast.</p>

      <div class="milestone-panel">
        <h3>Our first Dormition</h3>
        <div id="dormition-fields"></div>
      </div>
    </article>

