    <div id="opane-prayer" class="opane">
      <h3>The prayer corner</h3>
      <p class="view-note">This companion links to prayers rather than reprinting them. Translations of the services are owned by their translators, and more importantly, the wording your parish uses is the wording worth learning. Your prayer rule itself belongs with your priest — what follows is furniture, not a rule.</p>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>A simple daily shape</h3>
        <p>Many households begin with something like this, morning and evening, adjusted by their priest:</p>
        <ol class="rule-list">
          <li><strong>Stand before your icons.</strong> Light the lamp or a candle if you keep one.</li>
          <li><strong>The Trisagion Prayers</strong> — the Church&rsquo;s ordinary opening, from &ldquo;Holy God&rdquo; through the Our Father.</li>
          <li><strong>Psalm 50 (51)</strong> — the psalm of repentance, read daily in the Church&rsquo;s own services.</li>
          <li><strong>Remember the day</strong> — today&rsquo;s saints and readings are on the Today screen.</li>
          <li><strong>Your own words</strong> — the people you carry, by name. The Parish tab keeps your commemoration list.</li>
          <li><strong>The Jesus Prayer</strong> — see below.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="small">The full texts, with music and audio, are on the official
          <a href="https://dcs.goarch.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GOARCH Digital Chant Stand</a> —
          including <a href="https://dcs.goarch.org/goa/dcs/servicesindex.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">every day&rsquo;s services by date</a>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>The Jesus Prayer</h3>
        <p class="jesus-prayer">Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me, a sinner.</p>
        <p class="small">One sentence, drawn from the Gospels, prayed continually by Orthodox Christians for many centuries. It needs no book and no screen. If you use a prayer rope, the counter below can stand in when the rope is not in your pocket &mdash; it keeps no history and no score.</p>
        <div class="rope">
          <div class="rope-target" role="group" aria-label="Prayer rope counter">
            <button class="chip chip-sm rope-goal is-on" data-goal="33">33</button>
            <button class="chip chip-sm rope-goal" data-goal="50">50</button>
            <button class="chip chip-sm rope-goal" data-goal="100">100</button>
          </div>
          <button id="rope-btn" class="rope-btn" aria-describedby="rope-note">
            <span id="rope-count" aria-live="polite">0</span>
            <span class="rope-sub">tap with each prayer</span>
          </button>
          <div class="btn-row rope-row">
            <button class="btn btn-print" id="rope-reset">Begin again</button>
          </div>
          <p class="small" id="rope-note">Nothing here is saved or tracked. When you close the app, the count is gone &mdash; as it should be.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>The Paraklesis</h3>
        <p>The supplicatory canon to the Theotokos, served in parishes throughout the Dormition Fast &mdash; the evenings of this fast are largely built around it. Parish schedules vary; the Journey tab has a place to write yours.</p>
        <p><a href="https://dcs.goarch.org/goa/dcs/p/b/ho/ho23/gr-en/bk.ho.ho23.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Small Paraklesis &mdash; complete official text, Greek and English (Digital Chant Stand)</a></p>
        <p class="small">Reading it once at home before your first service helps enormously &mdash; the refrains repeat, and children catch them fast.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Psalms for the season</h3>
        <p>Citations only &mdash; read them from your own Bible or psalter. Orthodox books number the Psalms by the Greek Septuagint; most English Bibles use the Hebrew numbering, usually one higher. Both are given.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Psalm 50 (51)</strong> &mdash; repentance; the daily psalm of the Church&rsquo;s morning prayer</li>
          <li><strong>Psalm 26 (27)</strong> &mdash; courage before something new: fitting before a Chrismation</li>
          <li><strong>Psalm 33 (34)</strong> &mdash; thanksgiving; sung at the end of the Liturgy in many parishes</li>
          <li><strong>Psalm 102 (103)</strong> &mdash; blessing the Lord for everything; good for a feast evening</li>
          <li><strong>Psalm 120 (121)</strong> &mdash; for travel and for the night before August 6</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Listening</h3>
        <p>For chant while cooking the fast&rsquo;s suppers or driving to Paraklesis:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://dcs.goarch.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Digital Chant Stand</a> &mdash; the official scores and recordings for the actual services</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.ancientfaith.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ancient Faith</a> &mdash; streaming Orthodox music and podcasts (a supplemental source, not Archdiocesan)</li>
        </ul>
